When to Use Binary Search

On Sorted Arrays: Works best when the dataset is already sorted or can be sorted once and then searched multiple times.

For Fast Lookups: Ideal for searching large static datasets like firmware configuration tables, LUTs (Look-Up Tables), or indexed databases.

Low Memory Environments: Since it only requires O(1) extra space, it’s great for embedded systems where memory is limited.

Range Queries: Can be extended for upper/lower bound searches, useful for applications like BMS threshold lookups in automotive software.

Where It’s Used
Databases (e.g., indexing for fast record retrieval)
Game engines (e.g., spatial partitioning for rendering optimizations)
Machine learning models (e.g., searching through sorted feature sets)
Firmware (e.g., finding predefined values in sensor calibration data)

Where It Won’t Work
Unsorted Data → Needs sorting first, making it inefficient for dynamic datasets.
Linked Lists → Inefficient, as linked lists don’t allow O(1) index access.
Highly Dynamic Data → If data is constantly changing, maintaining sorted order can be expensive.